And the case no vector covers, because a corpus of malformed bytes has to
stop somewhere: 200 nested start tags, past the 100 the skipper allows.
AK_NEST sets the depth. At 200 the bound is what makes the rejection say
ErrDepth rather than ErrTruncated -- an unbounded skipper still rejects
this one, for the wrong reason. Deep enough and an unbounded skipper does
not reject at all: it overflows the stack, which .NET cannot catch.
  err = -8, ErrDepth, and the process is still here
  Google.Protobuf: rejected, InvalidProtocolBufferException

The same at 200,000, which is past where an unbounded skipper aborts:
  err = -8, ErrDepth, and the process is still here

